WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:03.261
- A lot of the time we do have good ideas,

00:00:03.261 --> 00:00:05.575
but we are afraid of failing.

00:00:05.575 --> 00:00:07.836
When I'm not joyful, I
look at what happened.

00:00:07.836 --> 00:00:11.118
We have a dedicated section
called Good News section.

00:00:11.118 --> 00:00:12.483
It only has good news.

00:00:12.483 --> 00:00:15.649
What I'm telling you is
to regularly disconnect

00:00:15.649 --> 00:00:20.249
from technology, to regularly
unplug and recharge.

00:00:20.249 --> 00:00:22.410
In the end, it's about how we feel inside

00:00:22.410 --> 00:00:25.082
and what is it that nourishes us.

00:00:25.082 --> 00:00:28.250
Socrates said, "Practice death daily."

00:00:28.250 --> 00:00:31.085
It's perfectly okay to be
upset and angry and irritated.

00:00:31.085 --> 00:00:33.147
But how long do you stay on it?

00:00:33.147 --> 00:00:35.211
Well, first of all, I prioritize sleep.

00:00:35.211 --> 00:00:38.377
It was when he was in meditation

00:00:38.377 --> 00:00:42.833
that often a lot of his best
ideas for Apple came to him.

00:00:42.833 --> 00:00:47.609
Success, the way we've defined
it, is no longer sustainable.

00:00:47.609 --> 00:00:50.472
- She's a Greek-American
author, syndicated columnist,

00:00:50.472 --> 00:00:51.928
and occasional actress.

00:00:51.928 --> 00:00:53.573
She's the co-founder and editor-in-chief

00:00:53.573 --> 00:00:54.984
of The Huffington Post.

00:00:54.984 --> 00:00:59.120
As of 2014, she's listed as
the 52nd most powerful woman

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:01.191
in the world by Forbes magazine.

00:01:01.191 --> 00:01:03.638
She's Arianna Huffington and
here are her top ten rules

00:01:03.638 --> 00:01:04.628
for success.

00:01:04.628 --> 00:01:07.829
(airplane taking off)

00:01:07.829 --> 00:01:10.493
- A lot of the time,
we do have good ideas,

00:01:10.493 --> 00:01:12.717
but we are afraid of failing.

00:01:12.717 --> 00:01:14.972
I think especially for women.

00:01:14.972 --> 00:01:18.253
And I see that in my daughters
and my daughters' friends.

00:01:18.253 --> 00:01:21.811
Fear of failure is what
stops us more than anything.

00:01:21.811 --> 00:01:22.676
- [Interviewer] Yeah, I agree.

00:01:22.676 --> 00:01:25.843
- I was like it in that I had a mother

00:01:28.554 --> 00:01:33.016
who was full of sort of
this colloquial wisdom.

00:01:33.016 --> 00:01:36.367
And she used to say to me,
"Failure is not the opposite

00:01:36.367 --> 00:01:39.293
"of success, it's a
stepping stone to success."

00:01:39.293 --> 00:01:41.814
There's nobody who has
not failed along the way.

00:01:41.814 --> 00:01:45.903
So I think it's very important
for young women especially

00:01:45.903 --> 00:01:49.551
as they are starting in
life to recognize that.

00:01:49.551 --> 00:01:53.551
Because otherwise they only
see people's successes.

00:01:53.551 --> 00:01:56.821
So when I speak, I speak of my failures.

00:01:56.821 --> 00:01:58.321
One entry point is

00:02:00.048 --> 00:02:01.581
bringing joy back

00:02:01.581 --> 00:02:03.775
into our everyday lives.

00:02:03.775 --> 00:02:07.942
Because so many of us, and I
speak as a complete culprit,

00:02:09.471 --> 00:02:11.888
as like a certifiable type A,

00:02:14.229 --> 00:02:16.729
workaholic, driven personality

00:02:17.642 --> 00:02:20.218
who basically felt my job was about

00:02:20.218 --> 00:02:22.650
getting it all done,
getting it done in time,

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:25.235
getting it accomplished.

00:02:25.235 --> 00:02:27.755
Joy, who has time for joy?

00:02:27.755 --> 00:02:30.866
And now I feel that not at all.

00:02:30.866 --> 00:02:35.369
That it's like when I'm not
joyful, I look at what happened.

00:02:35.369 --> 00:02:37.730
I don't mean joyful like
there are no challenges,

00:02:37.730 --> 00:02:38.944
problems, et cetera.

00:02:38.944 --> 00:02:41.225
I mean like when I'm not
like, "Hey, this is great.

00:02:41.225 --> 00:02:42.938
"I'm blessed. I'm doing something I love.

00:02:42.938 --> 00:02:44.188
"I'm grateful."

00:02:45.681 --> 00:02:50.152
When I don't have that
feeling, I know I'm off.

00:02:50.152 --> 00:02:51.953
I need to course correct.

00:02:51.953 --> 00:02:54.592
So joy, kind of really
checking in and seeing

00:02:54.592 --> 00:02:56.345
how am I doing.

00:02:56.345 --> 00:02:58.658
You don't even have to check in

00:02:58.658 --> 00:03:02.090
because you know when you are in the flow,

00:03:02.090 --> 00:03:04.233
in the joy and when you're not.

00:03:04.233 --> 00:03:08.241
What I tell everyone who
works with us is that

00:03:08.241 --> 00:03:12.081
all this stuff that we've
learned, we now need to apply

00:03:12.081 --> 00:03:14.331
to actually bringing people

00:03:15.797 --> 00:03:18.079
to see how they can live
their lives in a better way

00:03:18.079 --> 00:03:19.967
and make the world better.

00:03:19.967 --> 00:03:23.304
So these things don't have to be boring.

00:03:23.304 --> 00:03:25.489
They can actually be really exciting.

00:03:25.489 --> 00:03:29.552
We have a dedicated section
called Good News section.

00:03:29.552 --> 00:03:31.607
It only has good news.

00:03:31.607 --> 00:03:34.145
It's one of our most popular sections.

00:03:34.145 --> 00:03:37.145
(audience applause)

00:03:39.303 --> 00:03:41.220
And I think part of it

00:03:43.229 --> 00:03:45.167
is because of the zeitgeist.

00:03:45.167 --> 00:03:46.761
And I really believe in the zeitgeist.

00:03:46.761 --> 00:03:48.744
And I know it's a terrible German word,

00:03:48.744 --> 00:03:53.070
but there is such a
thing as the zeitgeist,

00:03:53.070 --> 00:03:54.609
you know the spirit of the times.

00:03:54.609 --> 00:03:56.696
The spirit of the times is changing.

00:03:56.696 --> 00:04:00.560
You see now many brands
want to be part of wellness

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:01.545
for example.

00:04:01.545 --> 00:04:06.105
When I decided to launch
The Huffington Post,

00:04:06.105 --> 00:04:09.377
it was an expensive operation.

00:04:09.377 --> 00:04:11.583
I wanted to be free.

00:04:11.583 --> 00:04:13.056
I was absolutely committed to that.

00:04:13.056 --> 00:04:15.031
I didn't want anybody
to have to pay for it

00:04:15.031 --> 00:04:18.976
because I wanted everybody
to be able to access it.

00:04:18.976 --> 00:04:21.728
I never thought it would be sponsored.

00:04:21.728 --> 00:04:24.992
When we announced that we
are going to launch it at CS,

00:04:24.992 --> 00:04:28.025
we had brands competing to sponsor it.

00:04:28.025 --> 00:04:31.392
It ended up being sponsored
by Weight Watchers

00:04:31.392 --> 00:04:35.559
because of the connection
between stress and weight.

00:04:37.608 --> 00:04:40.471
I really believe that if
we set up in the media

00:04:40.471 --> 00:04:42.999
to do the right thing,
and do it effectively,

00:04:42.999 --> 00:04:44.736
and do it smartly, and do it in a way

00:04:44.736 --> 00:04:48.584
that draws people to it
not in a didactic way,

00:04:48.584 --> 00:04:51.417
then the monetization will follow.

00:04:52.552 --> 00:04:56.293
If you look around you, you
see leaders in positions

00:04:56.293 --> 00:05:00.263
of power in politics,
in media, in business.

00:05:00.263 --> 00:05:03.839
All of them with high IQs, great degrees

00:05:03.839 --> 00:05:06.006
making terrible decisions.

00:05:07.585 --> 00:05:10.668
What is missing is not IQ but wisdom.

00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:14.161
And today it's getting
harder and harder to tap

00:05:14.161 --> 00:05:18.328
into our own wisdom because
we are all so hyper connected

00:05:19.376 --> 00:05:22.752
to our devices, our
screens, our social media

00:05:22.752 --> 00:05:25.615
that we're having a
hard time disconnecting

00:05:25.615 --> 00:05:29.681
from technology and
reconnecting with ourselves.

00:05:29.681 --> 00:05:33.848
Your very own, very wise Smith
sophomore, Erin McDaniel,

00:05:35.056 --> 00:05:37.798
wrote in the Sophian about
her decision to disconnect

00:05:37.798 --> 00:05:40.504
from all her social media.

00:05:40.504 --> 00:05:43.689
"We have eschewed," she said,
"real social connections

00:05:43.689 --> 00:05:47.183
"in favor of superficial
technology-bridged ones.

00:05:47.183 --> 00:05:49.362
"We have become, in many cases,

00:05:49.362 --> 00:05:53.609
"nearly as socially
robotic as our computers."

00:05:53.609 --> 00:05:56.713
Now, you don't have the
head of a digital company

00:05:56.713 --> 00:06:01.145
telling you to completely
disconnect from technology.

00:06:01.145 --> 00:06:04.433
What I'm telling you is
to regularly disconnect

00:06:04.433 --> 00:06:08.658
from technology, to
regularly unplug and recharge

00:06:08.658 --> 00:06:11.049
in order to reconnect with ourselves

00:06:11.049 --> 00:06:13.961
and our own deepest wisdom.

00:06:13.961 --> 00:06:18.046
It was actually at
Cambridge when I discovered

00:06:18.046 --> 00:06:21.304
the Cambridge Union which
was a debating society.

00:06:21.304 --> 00:06:25.318
And I fell in love with the
spectacle of people's minds

00:06:25.318 --> 00:06:27.465
and hearts being moved by words.

00:06:27.465 --> 00:06:28.618
And I said I want to do that.

00:06:28.618 --> 00:06:29.846
And I was terrible.

00:06:29.846 --> 00:06:34.088
When I first stood up to speak,
I was literally laughed at

00:06:34.088 --> 00:06:37.033
especially with my thick Greek accent

00:06:37.033 --> 00:06:39.250
which was even thicker than now.

00:06:39.250 --> 00:06:42.913
I had to read every word,
but at every debate,

00:06:42.913 --> 00:06:44.521
I would get up.

00:06:44.521 --> 00:06:46.184
Often they would call on me at midnight

00:06:46.184 --> 00:06:49.866
after they had exhausted
calling on everyone else.

00:06:49.866 --> 00:06:52.973
And I was just determined
to learn to speak.

00:06:52.973 --> 00:06:56.224
It's still my favorite thing even though

00:06:56.224 --> 00:06:59.170
obviously I spend a lot of time online

00:06:59.170 --> 00:07:01.801
and running a digital company.

00:07:01.801 --> 00:07:03.450
And I've written 14 books.

00:07:03.450 --> 00:07:05.746
My favorite thing is speaking.

00:07:05.746 --> 00:07:08.129
In the end, it's about how we feel inside

00:07:08.129 --> 00:07:10.771
and what is it that nourishes us.

00:07:10.771 --> 00:07:14.271
And it may not be what our society values.

00:07:16.497 --> 00:07:18.255
And that's why a lot of ...

00:07:18.255 --> 00:07:21.060
Increasingly we see millennials especially

00:07:21.060 --> 00:07:24.643
turning down the jobs
with the big salaries

00:07:25.820 --> 00:07:28.323
and doing something
like becoming a teacher

00:07:28.323 --> 00:07:32.490
in an inner city school
because that's what fuels them

00:07:33.427 --> 00:07:36.643
in a positive way and that's
what touches their heart.

00:07:36.643 --> 00:07:38.810
So when we dare to do that

00:07:39.980 --> 00:07:44.243
instead of living someone
else's life really,

00:07:44.243 --> 00:07:47.108
it's dramatic how everything changes.

00:07:47.108 --> 00:07:50.866
Socrates said, "Practice death daily."

00:07:50.866 --> 00:07:53.203
He didn't mean that in a morbid sense.

00:07:53.203 --> 00:07:57.028
He meant that if we
really consciously realize

00:07:57.028 --> 00:08:01.052
and remember every day that
we're all going to die,

00:08:01.052 --> 00:08:03.996
then we live life differently.

00:08:03.996 --> 00:08:06.850
Because first of all we
don't sweat the small stuff.

00:08:06.850 --> 00:08:11.019
When it comes to death,
everything is small stuff.

00:08:11.019 --> 00:08:14.851
It's the same thing
that the Romans thought.

00:08:14.851 --> 00:08:18.443
You know they would carve
on statues and on trees:

00:08:18.443 --> 00:08:21.276
"MM" memento mori, remember death.

00:08:23.031 --> 00:08:24.028
It's one of the things ...

00:08:24.028 --> 00:08:26.738
We actually have now a dedicated reporter

00:08:26.738 --> 00:08:30.051
on The Huffington Post covering death.

00:08:30.051 --> 00:08:34.130
Because again, that's again a
huge silence in our culture.

00:08:34.130 --> 00:08:36.731
But the thing that is the
absolutely inevitable part

00:08:36.731 --> 00:08:39.994
of our lives is not being
covered by the media.

00:08:39.994 --> 00:08:43.419
The Columbia Journalism Review,
his first name is (mumbles)

00:08:43.419 --> 00:08:45.427
if you can't remember his last name.

00:08:45.427 --> 00:08:47.802
He's doing amazing things around death

00:08:47.802 --> 00:08:50.357
in our religion section, which is next

00:08:50.357 --> 00:08:52.660
to our science section,
because increasingly

00:08:52.660 --> 00:08:55.115
we're trying to see how
the two are not opposed

00:08:55.115 --> 00:08:56.069
to each other.

00:08:56.069 --> 00:08:59.244
Dogmas may be opposed, but
the heart of a religion

00:08:59.244 --> 00:09:00.827
is very connected to the heart

00:09:00.827 --> 00:09:03.658
of huge scientific discoveries.

00:09:03.658 --> 00:09:06.408
So my point, to go back to death,

00:09:07.953 --> 00:09:10.893
is that if we make it part of life,

00:09:10.893 --> 00:09:14.514
it will make us live life differently.

00:09:14.514 --> 00:09:16.768
And so when the time comes for us to die,

00:09:16.768 --> 00:09:18.488
we will be prepared for it.

00:09:18.488 --> 00:09:20.442
- [Interviewer] Yeah,
because we practiced it.

00:09:20.442 --> 00:09:22.234
- Because we'll have
practiced it every day.

00:09:22.234 --> 00:09:24.651
And my advice to everybody is

00:09:26.798 --> 00:09:28.425
look at a child

00:09:28.425 --> 00:09:31.553
and see how a child handles
being upset or angry.

00:09:31.553 --> 00:09:34.320
I don't believe in growing a thick skin.

00:09:34.320 --> 00:09:37.274
I think that stops you
from really being permeable

00:09:37.274 --> 00:09:41.144
and feeling everything to the fullest.

00:09:41.144 --> 00:09:45.779
But a child will get
really upset and really cry

00:09:45.779 --> 00:09:47.320
and then like five minutes later

00:09:47.320 --> 00:09:49.448
it's as though nothing has happened.

00:09:49.448 --> 00:09:50.281
That's what I love.

00:09:50.281 --> 00:09:51.631
I love to live life like that.

00:09:51.631 --> 00:09:55.520
It's perfectly okay to be
upset and angry and irritated.

00:09:55.520 --> 00:09:57.757
But how long do you stay on it?

00:09:57.757 --> 00:09:58.943
That's for me the key.

00:09:58.943 --> 00:10:01.279
It's like, can you say "next" and move on

00:10:01.279 --> 00:10:02.621
and not hold grudges.

00:10:02.621 --> 00:10:04.272
That's the other key. Oh my God.

00:10:04.272 --> 00:10:05.884
Holding grudges is the worst.

00:10:05.884 --> 00:10:07.680
- It's a big part of
the political landscape.

00:10:07.680 --> 00:10:09.471
Did you have to learn
to do that, to let go?

00:10:09.471 --> 00:10:12.817
- Oh yeah, I worked at it,
and I really believe in it.

00:10:12.817 --> 00:10:16.311
The most draining thing
we can do in our lives

00:10:16.311 --> 00:10:19.261
is hold onto something other than upset

00:10:19.261 --> 00:10:21.094
or anger or judgments.

00:10:23.040 --> 00:10:26.311
I like what the Bible says,
"The evil of today is enough."

00:10:26.311 --> 00:10:27.961
"Let the dead bury the dead."

00:10:27.961 --> 00:10:31.857
Move on, that's my advice for everybody.

00:10:31.857 --> 00:10:35.008
Well, first of all, I prioritize sleep.

00:10:35.008 --> 00:10:39.327
That means saying no to
things you want to do.

00:10:39.327 --> 00:10:40.202
It's not easy.

00:10:40.202 --> 00:10:42.373
- [Interviewer] That is hard,
I think the hardest thing.

00:10:42.373 --> 00:10:45.404
- Last night Sheryl
Sandberg interviewed me

00:10:45.404 --> 00:10:47.853
at the San Francisco Symphony Hall.

00:10:47.853 --> 00:10:49.839
And then I had to sign books.

00:10:49.839 --> 00:10:53.022
I would have loved to go
and have dinner with her.

00:10:53.022 --> 00:10:54.522
But I went to bed.

00:10:57.887 --> 00:11:02.461
Because if had gone out to
dinner and hadn't gotten my

00:11:02.461 --> 00:11:04.613
at least seven hours sleep that I need

00:11:04.613 --> 00:11:07.822
and had to get up early to do
TV, I would not be enjoying

00:11:07.822 --> 00:11:10.495
being here with you now,
and I'm really enjoying it.

00:11:10.495 --> 00:11:12.727
And I'm 100% present and I'm not tired.

00:11:12.727 --> 00:11:15.662
And I hate being tired
more than I hate anything.

00:11:15.662 --> 00:11:18.455
If you really want to be creative,

00:11:18.455 --> 00:11:21.288
just go with Steve Jobs' biography

00:11:24.111 --> 00:11:26.248
by Walter Isaacson

00:11:26.248 --> 00:11:30.694
in which Steve Jobs says
how it was when he was

00:11:30.694 --> 00:11:34.289
in meditation that often
a lot of his best ideas

00:11:34.289 --> 00:11:36.824
for Apple came to him.

00:11:36.824 --> 00:11:40.744
He calls it being able to
listen to the whisperings.

00:11:40.744 --> 00:11:43.983
But otherwise we are really too busy

00:11:43.983 --> 00:11:47.064
and there's too much static in
our lives to listen to them.

00:11:47.064 --> 00:11:48.576
- Yeah, yeah, beautiful, yeah.

00:11:48.576 --> 00:11:50.373
- And Bill Gates, you
know he would have his

00:11:50.373 --> 00:11:54.153
what do you call think weeks,
when he would take a week off

00:11:54.153 --> 00:11:58.153
without any devices and
go to a cabin and think.

00:11:59.984 --> 00:12:03.234
And again, to go back to another Greek.

00:12:04.348 --> 00:12:06.931
I always go back to the Greeks.

00:12:07.809 --> 00:12:11.560
Who came up with his
Eureka moment in the bath.

00:12:11.560 --> 00:12:14.534
How often don't we have
our most creative ideas

00:12:14.534 --> 00:12:16.554
in the shower or the bath.

00:12:16.554 --> 00:12:19.896
At the moment, our
society's notion of success

00:12:19.896 --> 00:12:24.063
is largely composed of two
parts: money and power.

00:12:25.192 --> 00:12:29.025
In fact, success, money,
and power have practically

00:12:29.025 --> 00:12:30.525
become synonymous.

00:12:31.455 --> 00:12:35.622
But it's time for a third
metric beyond money and power,

00:12:36.575 --> 00:12:39.408
one founded on well-being, wisdom,

00:12:40.475 --> 00:12:43.521
our ability to wonder and to give back.

00:12:43.521 --> 00:12:46.521
(audience applause)

00:12:48.657 --> 00:12:50.808
You know, money and power by themselves

00:12:50.808 --> 00:12:53.113
are like a two-legged stool.

00:12:53.113 --> 00:12:56.113
You can balance on them for a while,

00:12:56.113 --> 00:12:59.334
but eventually you're
going to topple over.

00:12:59.334 --> 00:13:02.863
And more and more people,
very successful people,

00:13:02.863 --> 00:13:05.196
are toppling over every day.

00:13:06.312 --> 00:13:09.788
Basically, success the
way we've defined it

00:13:09.788 --> 00:13:12.439
is no longer sustainable.

00:13:12.439 --> 00:13:15.222
It's not sustainable for human beings.

00:13:15.222 --> 00:13:18.140
It's not sustainable for the planet.

00:13:18.140 --> 00:13:19.055
- Thank you so much for watching.

00:13:19.055 --> 00:13:21.692
I made this video because Alf asked me to.

00:13:21.692 --> 00:13:23.700
So if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:13:23.700 --> 00:13:25.452
that you want me to profile next,

00:13:25.452 --> 00:13:27.259
leave it in the comments below
and I'll see what I can do.

00:13:27.259 --> 00:13:29.685
I'd also love to know which
of Huffington's top ten rules

00:13:29.685 --> 00:13:32.285
meant the most to you,
had the biggest impact.

00:13:32.285 --> 00:13:33.436
Leave it in the comments below

00:13:33.436 --> 00:13:35.014
and I'll join in the discussion.

00:13:35.014 --> 00:13:36.156
Thank you so much for watching.

00:13:36.156 --> 00:13:39.656
Continue to believe and I'll see you soon.

